A||||5||Stroker||MY TURBO PROJECT
Z||000000||Stroker||08-19-99||10:44 PM||||Well guys and gals,I FINALLY found a good used turbo for MY project,so now I have somethin' of interest for all you turbo fans(batboy,you listenin'?). I got my turbo today for $75. It came off of the sister car to the T-Bird, the Cougar XR7.This turbo IS water cooled and it came off an 84 model Cougar.<p>It is the infamous Garrett T-3 and it will be getting a rebuild shortly,which should cost another $75.<p>I guess that while the 84 T-bird used the air cooled version, the people at Mercury went one better and opted for the water-cooled version(though I'm just guessin'):&gt<IMG SRC="/ubb/wink.gif" WIDTH=15 HEIGHT=15><p>Dennis Lagrua is one guy who I've talked to who is THOROUGHLY enjoying his ride!!!!!!!!! I only have about 6000 miles on my freshly rebuilt (and stroked,of course)3.2 v-6.I have the Federal Mogul hypereutectic pistons and 9.1:1 compression.I'll be starting the boost level at 5-7 psi. and will work up to what I feel comfortable with (but no more than 10 psi.).<br>This turbo also came with an adjustable wastegate so that's one less thing I'll have to modify.:&gt<IMG SRC="/ubb/wink.gif" WIDTH=15 HEIGHT=15><br>I'll keep you all up to date on my progress and pass along any and all helpful tips that I come across for future reference.I think this is the project I've looked most forward to,so I know it'll be a blast!!!!<br>SEE YA , Stroker
Z||000001||batboy||08-19-99||11:53 PM||||I'm listening! <IMG SRC="/ubb/smile.gif" WIDTH=15 HEIGHT=15> 
Z||000002||theogre||08-20-99||12:44 AM||||9+:1 curb compression? I'd start at 3-4 PSI boost and work up. You are likely to run into problems with getting good enough gas really fast. Especially without an inter-cooler. Tubochargers really heat the air.<p>Many turbo/super motors have a curb compression of as low as 8:1. sometimes even lower with big boost. There are very good reasons for that. Like the fact they run on pump gas, and don't explode.<p>((engines with higher than 9.3:1 static compression offten require 92+ octain fuel as it is. Otherwise they ping and pre all over the place. Usually end up breaking something.))
Z||000003||Kage86GT4SP||08-20-99||08:35 AM||||Stroker...<p>add me to your mailing list, would you?<p>I scored a T4 (TO4?) watercooled with wastegate and exhaust, in great condition... it came off a mitsubishi of all things... for 65$ yesterday... cause I'm a hankerin' to tbow.<p>I have a 4 sp, with down the road plans for a 5 speed (if I can find one for less then the fortune it seems most want around here) so I can't use the set up that Dennis LaGrua utilized... <p>http://members.xoom.com/dlagrua/turbo.htm <p>I just can see a mid-position turbo over the center of the bell housing not interfering with the shifter linkage (he's running an automatic).<p>But I can see the set up for placement used by turboconnection<p>http://www.turboconnection.com/showcase/turbofiero/turbofiero01.html<p>which is located just off the rear exhaust manifold.<p>Keep us posted, OK?  <p>K<p>
Z||000004||Kage86GT4SP||08-20-99||08:37 AM||||Dang it, that should read:<p>"I just canN'T see a mid-position turbo over the center of the bell housing not interfering with the shifter linkage (he's running an automatic)."<p><p>
Z||000005||Stroker||08-20-99||06:02 PM||||Hey Kage, it's good to see that I'm not alone in this "turbo quest":&gt<IMG SRC="/ubb/wink.gif" WIDTH=15 HEIGHT=15><br>I understand the frustration over the placement of the turbo just over the bell housing,but I THINK it might just work like that on the 4 and 5 speeds IF the placement is just right.<br>I'm running the 4 spd. also and I HAD cruise control but took it off to see what kind of room I'd have on that side for the turbo (this is where IRM and others put it on a manual trans).There is a canyon of room for it there, and I can get a cruise unit from JC Whitney that doesn't utilize a vacuum pod,so that should save some room.<p>There is a GREAT website by Ray Hall Turbocharging (just look him up by that name on your search engine)!!<br>His site has a terrific turbo sizing program that helps you build the car right! It even has a compression calculator to let you know what your comp. is after turbocharging and what octane requirements each application would need.<br>Check it out and keep me posted on you progress!!!!  <br>     <br>  Stroker
